From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cgroup: freezer: let frozen tasks be killed

A task frozen by the cgroup (v1) freezer sleeps uninterruptibly in
__refrigerator() and stays there with a pending SIGKILL until its cgroup
is thawed. Android kills frozen apps without thawing them: the framework
when it trims or force-stops them, init when zygote restarts, and on
this device the in-kernel low memory killer, for which frozen cached
apps are the first candidates. With the stock behaviour none of these
kills would free any memory; lowmem_shrink() would wait a second for a
victim that cannot die and then pick the same one again. The cgroup v2
freezer, which Android has used since Android 12, lets frozen tasks be
killed. Do the same here:

- freezing_slow_path(): the cgroup freezer no longer applies to a task
  that has a SIGKILL pending or whose thread group is exiting
  (SIGNAL_GROUP_EXIT, which complete_signal() sets together with the
  per-thread SIGKILL and which stays set after the thread has dequeued
  the signal, until do_exit() sets PF_NOFREEZE). Such a task leaves the
  refrigerator, is not sent there by freeze_task() or freezer_count(),
  and does not keep a cgroup from reaching FROZEN.
- __refrigerator(): a task that is frozen only by the cgroup freezer
  sleeps in TASK_KILLABLE, so that the SIGKILL wakes it
  (signal_wake_up(t, 1)). Signals that do not kill still do not wake it.

The system-wide freezer used for suspend is unchanged: while pm_freezing
or pm_nosig_freezing is set, freezing() is true for a killed task as
before and the sleep is uninterruptible as before. A cgroup-frozen task
that is killed during suspend wakes up once, sees the system-wide
freeze, goes back to sleep and dies when thaw_processes() wakes it. The
sleep state is chosen under freezer_lock so that the end of a
system-wide freeze cannot leave a cgroup-frozen task in the
uninterruptible sleep (see the comment in __refrigerator()). Tasks in
the root cgroup and kernel threads are not affected at all.

Every freezer cgroup except the root gets a file "freezer.killable"
that reads 1, so that userspace can tell this kernel from one where a
frozen task cannot be killed. The patched Android framework of this
project only enables the app freezer when it finds the file.

Android kernels that ran Android 11's v1 app freezer have a file of
that name too (seen in LineageOS/android_kernel_google_msm-4.14 and in
the 3.10 tree acroreiser/android_kernel_lenovo_a6010). There it is a
per-cgroup switch, off by default, and the kill is handled by sleeping
interruptibly and clearing TIF_SIGPENDING in the refrigerator loop, also
while the system is suspending. Here the behaviour cannot be turned off
(writing 1 to the file is accepted and does nothing, anything else is
-EINVAL), the sleep is killable so that no pending-signal flag has to be
touched, and the suspend freezer is kept out of it.

There is no upstream commit for this; the mainline v1 freezer still
holds killed tasks until the thaw.

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.
---
 kernel/cgroup_freezer.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 kernel/freezer.c        | 44 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 67 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/cgroup_freezer.c b/kernel/cgroup_freezer.c
index 845050ce..e26c9db0 100644
--- a/kernel/cgroup_freezer.c
+++ b/kernel/cgroup_freezer.c
@@ -470,6 +470,26 @@ static u64 freezer_parent_freezing_read(struct cgroup *cgroup, struct cftype *cf
 	return (bool)(freezer->state & CGROUP_FREEZING_PARENT);
 }
 
+/*
+ * "freezer.killable" reads 1: a frozen task of a freezer cgroup dies at once
+ * when it is killed (see cgroup_freezer_task_killed() in kernel/freezer.c),
+ * as with the cgroup v2 freezer.  Userspace that kills frozen processes
+ * without thawing them first can test for the file.  On Android kernels
+ * that have this file it is a per-cgroup switch that is off by default;
+ * here it cannot be turned off, so writing 1 is accepted and does nothing
+ * and writing anything else fails.
+ */
+static u64 freezer_killable_read(struct cgroup *cgroup, struct cftype *cft)
+{
+	return 1;
+}
+
+static int freezer_killable_write(struct cgroup *cgroup, struct cftype *cft,
+				  u64 val)
+{
+	return val == 1 ? 0 : -EINVAL;
+}
+
 static struct cftype files[] = {
 	{
 		.name = "state",
@@ -487,6 +507,12 @@ static struct cftype files[] = {
 		.flags = CFTYPE_NOT_ON_ROOT,
 		.read_u64 = freezer_parent_freezing_read,
 	},
+	{
+		.name = "killable",
+		.flags = CFTYPE_NOT_ON_ROOT,
+		.read_u64 = freezer_killable_read,
+		.write_u64 = freezer_killable_write,
+	},
 	{ }	/* terminate */
 };
 
diff --git a/kernel/freezer.c b/kernel/freezer.c
index 4ada72f5..bfc0a307 100644
--- a/kernel/freezer.c
+++ b/kernel/freezer.c
@@ -28,6 +28,20 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(pm_freezing);
 /* protects freezing and frozen transitions */
 static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(freezer_lock);
 
+/*
+ * A task that is being killed is neither sent to nor kept in the
+ * refrigerator by the cgroup freezer, so that killing a frozen process
+ * frees its memory at once instead of when the cgroup is thawed.
+ * SIGNAL_GROUP_EXIT covers the time after the task has dequeued its
+ * SIGKILL and before do_exit() sets PF_NOFREEZE.  The system-wide (PM)
+ * freezer is not affected, see freezing_slow_path().
+ */
+static bool cgroup_freezer_task_killed(struct task_struct *p)
+{
+	return __fatal_signal_pending(p) ||
+	       (p->signal->flags & SIGNAL_GROUP_EXIT);
+}
+
 /**
  * freezing_slow_path - slow path for testing whether a task needs to be frozen
  * @p: task to be tested
@@ -45,12 +59,15 @@ bool freezing_slow_path(struct task_struct *p)
 	if (test_thread_flag(TIF_MEMDIE))
 		return false;
 
-	if (pm_nosig_freezing || cgroup_freezing(p))
+	if (pm_nosig_freezing)
 		return true;
 
 	if (pm_freezing && !(p->flags & PF_KTHREAD))
 		return true;
 
+	if (cgroup_freezing(p) && !cgroup_freezer_task_killed(p))
+		return true;
+
 	return false;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(freezing_slow_path);
@@ -66,9 +83,30 @@ bool __refrigerator(bool check_kthr_stop)
 	pr_debug("%s entered refrigerator\n", current->comm);
 
 	for (;;) {
-		set_current_state(TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE);
-
 		spin_lock_irq(&freezer_lock);
+		/*
+		 * A task that is here only because of the cgroup freezer
+		 * sleeps killable: SIGKILL wakes it up, freezing() is then
+		 * false for it (see freezing_slow_path()) and it leaves to
+		 * die.  While a system-wide freeze is in progress every task
+		 * has to stay put, killed or not, so the sleep is
+		 * uninterruptible then, and a kill that arrives in the
+		 * meantime is acted upon when thaw_processes() wakes the
+		 * task up.
+		 *
+		 * The state is chosen under freezer_lock: thaw_processes()
+		 * clears the pm_* flags before it calls __thaw_task(), which
+		 * takes the lock and wakes every task that has PF_FROZEN set.
+		 * A task that saw the flags set here is therefore woken up
+		 * afterwards and chooses again.  When a system-wide freeze
+		 * starts after the flags were read, a killed task falls
+		 * through schedule() once and chooses again as well.
+		 */
+		if (pm_freezing || pm_nosig_freezing)
+			set_current_state(TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE);
+		else
+			set_current_state(TASK_KILLABLE);
+
 		current->flags |= PF_FROZEN;
 		if (!freezing(current) ||
 		    (check_kthr_stop && kthread_should_stop()))
